  "slug": "untitled-topic-af7b",
  "summary": "Simulation and theory studies now couple movement or social tie dynamics to demographic, epidemiological and evolutionary outcomes.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that network or spatial topology determines how changes in individual animals or hosts propagate to population and community outcomes. Simulations across 268 empirical animal social networks show that compensatory tie formation alters how individual loss reorganises social structure, so predicted consequences of losing an individual depend on whether remaining animals rewire. Host-parasite coevolution on spatial networks links degree heterogeneity to host resistance and spatial variation in parasite infectivity. A spatially explicit individual-based model shows that herbivore movement and landscape structure jointly shape resource depletion and population dynamics. A theoretical model adds that parasite-impaired host mobility reduces virulence through altered relatedness and host density, with feedbacks on prevalence. What is new is the explicit coupling of movement or tie dynamics to evolutionary and demographic outcomes. These items are largely simulation- or theory-based, so the conclusions remain model-dependent pending empirical testing.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
